Fix for clingox UPSTREAM-ISSUES U1: integer division traps abort the process.

gringo evaluates INT_MIN / -1 and INT_MIN \ -1 without a check, and the ground
term parser (clingo_parse_term) does not check \ for a zero divisor. On x86
these raise SIGFPE and kill the process, so any program or term text can abort
an application that embeds clingo. With this patch the three places that
evaluate a division treat these cases as undefined, like a division by zero:
the term parser fails with "parsing failed", and the grounder logs "operation
undefined" and drops the rule instance.

The grounder also divides when it matches a linear term such as -X+0 against a
value (LinearTerm::match and GLinearTerm::match): solving -1 * X = INT_MIN
traps the same way. There the patch reports no match, since no int X solves it.

Divisions that do not trap on x86 keep their values. On targets where
INT_MIN / -1 does not trap (AArch64 returns INT_MIN, and 0 for the remainder),
those results were undefined behaviour in C++ and are now undefined in gringo
too. Against clingo v5.8.2; suitable for upstream.

